{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790327893,"nonce":"3ULZcoGTysmkC5ZbB05R5QNUVX4=","title":"Lua C API as a ZRTL plugin linked into zylua","message":"zylua must pass the whole official 5.4 suite, including attrib.lua's C-module section (official/attrib.lua from line 79, `if not _port`), which loads libs/lib1, lib11, lib2, lib21 and lib2-v2 (from lib22.c) through package.loadlib (with \"*\"), package.cpath, the C and all-in-one searchers, luaopen_ names and the \"-\" version-suffix rule. Today zl_package_loadlib answers (nil, msg, \"absent\") and zl_require raises \"C modules cannot be loaded\". The suite is Lua 5.4.8's (closure.lua cites \"bug in 5.4.7\"); the 5.4.x ABI is the same across releases.\n\nDecision: the Lua C API (lua.h and lauxlib.h) is a ZRTL plugin crate owned by the frontend, statically linked into zylua with `linked-into-host`, never a dylib. The jump that implements lua_error happens only in C, and it crosses only C frames. Zyntax gets one language-neutral package, sdk/zrtl_native, and nothing in crates/compiler, crates/zyntax_embed, crates/runtime or sdk/zrtl names Lua.\n\nThis synthesises three designs (in-frontend Rust with asm, a Rust plugin crate with a Host vtable, and a C veneer over Rust cores). The C veneer wins on the two things that must be right: the error boundary and the variadics. Stable Rust cannot define variadic functions (lua_pushfstring, luaL_error, lua_gc) or take a va_list (lua_pushvfstring), and hand-written asm readers for four va_list ABIs are the riskiest code any design proposed. Writing the veneer in C also lets lauxlib.c be vendored unchanged, so every luaL_* message and behaviour is the reference's by construction.\n\n\n1. sdk/zrtl_native (new crate, Zyntax, language-neutral). Built with the cc crate from its build.rs; `links = \"zrtl_native\"` so dependents find include/zrtl_native.h through DEP_ZRTL_NATIVE_INCLUDE. Not built for wasm32.\n   - boundary.c: `zrtl_native_frame { jmp_buf jb; prev; status; value }`; `int zrtl_native_protect(frame **chain, int (*body)(void *), void *ctx, int *out)` returns 0 with body's result in *out, or the status a raise carried; `_Noreturn void zrtl_native_raise(frame **chain, int status, int value)` jumps to *chain. With an empty chain, raise returns ZRTL_NATIVE_NO_FRAME to its caller, which decides what to do. Unix uses _setjmp/_longjmp (no signal-mask syscalls). MSVC x64 (phase 3) zeroes `((_JUMP_BUFFER *)jb)-\u003eFrame` after setjmp, pinned by a static assert, so longjmp restores registers and never runs an SEH unwind over foreign frames or a fiber stack.\n   - Library: `open(path, Scope::Local | Scope::Global)` (dlopen RTLD_NOW with RTLD_LOCAL or RTLD_GLOBAL; LoadLibraryExW with LOAD_WITH_ALTERED_SEARCH_PATH), `symbol(name)`, the dlerror/FormatMessageW text. Handles are never closed.\n   - A build helper, `export_link_args(target, names)`: exported_symbols_list (with `_main`) on macOS, `--dynamic-list` on Linux, a .def file on Windows.\n   - A compiler test (crates/compiler): an indirect call whose function type uses CallingConvention::Default reaches a Rust `extern \"C\"` target with pointer, i64 and f64 arguments, on the interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM. It pins the invariant the C API rests on. Checked in the code: lowering maps Default to System; Cranelift indirect calls use the module's default convention (cranelift_backend.rs); the interpreter's CallIndirect on an address that is not one of its own functions calls it through a site thunk built by NativeSig::of_site, which has `fast: false`.\n   No collector change: collector::add_root_range, stack and fiber-stack scanning and interior pointers already exist.\n\n2. crates/zyntax_lua/capi (package zyntax_lua_capi, frontend-owned). Depends on zrtl and zrtl_native only; tags and runtime hooks arrive through `install`. Declares `linked-into-host = []`; zyntax_lua enables it, exactly as zrtl_io, zrtl_string and zrtl_math are linked.\n   - vendor/: lua.h, luaconf.h (stock), lauxlib.h, lualib.h and lauxlib.c from 5.4.8, with the MIT notice. Module authors and the fixture build compile against these headers.\n   - c/lapi.c: one C veneer per lua.h function. A veneer calls its Rust core (`zlc_*`); a core never raises and returns ZLC_OK, ZLC_RAISED or a result, and the veneer raises on ZLC_RAISED. Written in C: lua_pushfstring and lua_pushvfstring (the reference's %s %d %I %f %p %c %U %% rules, %f as \"%.14g\" plus \".0\" for integral values), lua_gc, lua_error, lua_callk, lua_pcallk, lua_yieldk, `invoke_body` (calls the lua_CFunction through its real prototype), the panic path, and stubs for anything not yet implemented, which raise \"lua_xxx is not provided by zylua yet\".\n   - c/internal.h: zlc_* prototypes and the State offset of `chain`, checked by a C static assert against a constant Rust exports.\n   - build.rs: compiles lapi.c and lauxlib.c into libzl_capi.a and generates the export name list from the vendored headers' LUA_API and LUALIB_API declarations, plus lua_ident. zylua's build.rs reads the same list.\n   - src/state.rs (State, Global, CFrame, stack, index rules), src/values.rs (tag reads, pushes), src/calls.rs (the thunk, call, pcall, yield), src/load.rs (loadlib, loadfunc, the handle cache), src/bridge.rs (the Bridge table), src/lib.rs (`zrtl_plugin!` named \"lua_capi\" with the `$LuaC$*` symbols, `install`, and `EXPORTS`).\n   - `static_plugin()` reads `EXPORTS`, a table of every exported function's address, through std::hint::black_box. That keeps every archive member and every function reachable from main on every linker, under -dead_strip, --gc-sections and LTO.\n\n3. crates/zyntax_lua. `src/library/capi.rs` holds the bridge entries in the library DSL and `bridge_program()`, a TypedProgram whose entries are the `lua$capi$*` functions. Also: the userdata kinds; the lazy coroutine State slot; require, loadlib and cpath in stdlib.rs; the scope rule; the export flags in build.rs; and the harness.\n\n\nC never sees a table layout, a shape or a dict. Every Lua-semantic operation goes through a Bridge: a `#[repr(C)]` table of `extern \"C\"` function pointers to library functions compiled from bridge_program(). The functions are thin wrappers over the library's own paths: zl_index, zl_setindex, zl_rawget_key, zl_rawset_key, zl_rawgeti, zl_rawseti, zl_next, zl_rawlen, zl_len, zl_concat, zl_rawequal, the arithmetic and comparison dispatch, zl_meta_of and the per-type metatables (zl_type_meta and zl_set_type_meta), zl_call_packed, zl_pcall/zl_xpcall, zl_raise_value, zl_take_pending, zl_load_raw, zl_globals_value, zl_registry, zl_co_current, zl_co_main_thread, zb_func_new, and multi-value packing (zl_pack, zl_none, zl_values). Rules:\n- Signatures use only pointers, i64 and f64. No bool, no narrow integers, no aggregates, so the Apple arm64 extension rules and the aggregate split between the tiers (e1efccb) cannot reach it.\n- On the first successful native open, `install`'s resolver runs `runtime().join_typed_program(bridge_program(), entries)`, the path load_chunk already uses, and fills the table from `function_pointer`. Joined code is compiled at the baseline and stays there, so the addresses are stable.\n- A version word and the entry count are checked at install, and a unit test compares the entry list with the Bridge fields, so drift between the snapshot library and the plugin fails loudly.\n- The `$LuaC$*` ZRTL symbols are reached only from library HIR; the `lua_*` names never enter the JIT's symbol table.\n\n\n- lua_State: a pool block (zrtl::heap, which install_sdk_allocator points at the pool). L points LUA_EXTRASPACE bytes into it, so lua_getextraspace is valid memory. `#[repr(C)] State { extra, chain, stack, top, cap, ci, thread, global, nny, yields, status }`. There is one State per Lua thread. The main State is rooted through `add_root_range` on one static word. A coroutine's State is a boxed pointer in its record, appended lazily at a fixed index past DEAD_HOOK (padding with nil), so a coroutine that never runs C code has the same record as today. It lives exactly as long as its coroutine.\n- API stack: a pool block of `*const DynamicBox` words, with nil as the null word. It grows by allocating, copying and swapping. Vacated slots are zeroed on pop and settop, which keeps the pool invariant that unwritten words are zero. LUA_MINSTACK is free at every C entry; lua_checkstack grows up to LUAI_MAXSTACK and returns 0 beyond it.\n- CFrame lives on the thunk's Rust stack frame: `{ prev, base, func_rec, caller_line, caller_chunk, yields_at_entry }`. stack[base-1] holds the running function value, as in the reference.\n- Index rules follow lapi.c index2value: positive is base+idx-1 (none above top); negative above LUA_REGISTRYINDEX is top+idx; LUA_REGISTRYINDEX (-1001000) is the registry; below it are the running C closure's upvalue cells, which lua_replace and lua_copy can write.\n- Scalars go through zrtl tags, as host.rs reads them: nil, BOOL, I64 (also accept the UINT category), F64. lua_tointegerx converts a float only when exact, and strings by host::parse_numeral. lua_version returns 504; LUAL_NUMSIZES is 136.\n- Strings: pushes build zrtl strings with a zero byte after the data (TEXT only when UTF-8). lua_tolstring returns header+16. When the string's storage does not provably hold a terminator, the value in its slot or upvalue cell is replaced once by an equal, terminated copy. The manual guarantees the pointer only while the value stays on the stack, so the swap keeps the contract; only %p and lua_topointer on strings can see it. A number is converted in place, as the manual says. Phase 2 adds a TERMINATED header flag to sdk/zrtl (also bf3b723's header work) so the copy becomes rare.\n- Tables: always through the Bridge (see above). lua_createtable passes its hints as a reserve.\n- C functions: an ordinary FUNC_TAG record `[code = the thunk (CODE_TAG box), arity = VARIADIC_ARITY, cfn (CODE_TAG box), upvalue1..n]`, built by the bridge over zb_func_new. Light C functions (n = 0) are interned in a Lua table held by Global and keyed by the cfn address as an integer, so pushing the same function twice gives rawequal values. lua_iscfunction and lua_tocfunction compare the code word with the thunk.\n- Full userdata: USERDATA_KIND = 5 (kinds 0 to 4 are taken in library/mod.rs), a pool block `{ meta, nuv, size, uv[nuv] }` followed by a payload aligned to 16. lua_touserdata returns the payload, an interior pointer. Light userdata: LIGHT_KIND = 6, whose data word is the pointer; equality and table-key hashing use that word. The library learns both kinds in type (\"userdata\"), tostring, equality, zl_meta_of, getmetatable, rawlen and the instance hooks. Those branches sit after the table and string tests, so existing paths pay nothing.\n- Threads: the coroutine record. lua_tothread creates the State on demand; lua_pushthread pushes L.thread, or the main thread value, and returns 1 on main.\n- Registry: the library's zl_registry global, the table debug.getregistry returns, seeded at install with [1] the main thread, [2] zl_globals_value(), _LOADED = package.loaded, _PRELOAD = package.preload and _CLIBS. luaL_ref, luaL_newmetatable and the rest come from the vendored lauxlib.c unchanged.\n- lua_newstate and luaL_newstate return NULL. A second independent state is not supported.\n\n\nLua to C. The record's code is `capi_call: extern \"C\" fn(rec, args) -\u003e *const DynamicBox`, the variadic function-value shape the library already calls (zl_apply_packed passes a boxed tuple; zb_call_n passes a boxed list; the thunk accepts both). No tier ever calls a foreign pointer: the C function is called only from C, through its real prototype. The thunk:\n1. Finds the State: main when zl_co_current is nil, else the record's slot, created on first use. It counts the C call against the C-stack limit.\n2. Pushes a CFrame, then the arguments, read in place.\n3. Calls `zlc_invoke(L, cfn, \u0026n)`, which runs `zrtl_native_protect(\u0026L-\u003echain, invoke_body, ...)`.\n4. Normal return (or a RETURN status): the top n slots become the result: zl_none, the value itself, or a tuple. RAISED: returns nil with the error pending, and the caller's existing pending check takes over (the zl_call_* family is already fallible).\n5. Restores top and ci and zeroes the vacated slots.\n\nIt is `extern \"C\"`, so a panic aborts instead of unwinding.\n\nC to Lua. lua_callk: the core pops the function and arguments into a pool list, calls the bridge over zl_call_packed (counted, `__call` resolved), and returns ZLC_RAISED if an error is pending; the veneer then raises. Otherwise it pushes the results adjusted to nresults. lua_pcallk calls a bridge entry sharing zl_pcall and zl_xpcall's logic, so message handlers and \"error in error handling\" behave identically. It returns LUA_ERRRUN or LUA_ERRERR with the error value pushed. A pending closing marker (CLOSING_KIND) is never caught: the veneer raises it on. Every other API function that can run Lua code (the get and set family, arith, compare, concat, len, next) follows the same core-returns-RAISED rule.\n\n\n- The jump happens only in C. lua_error calls zlc_error, which sets the pending error through zl_raise_value (the first error stands; nil becomes NIL_ERROR_KIND). The veneer then calls zrtl_native_raise(\u0026L-\u003echain, RAISED, 0). luaL_error, luaL_argerror, luaL_typeerror and every luaL_check* are vendored lauxlib and end in lua_error.\n- What a jump crosses: the veneer, lauxlib frames, the module's own C frames (including direct C-to-C calls such as lib11 calling lib1_export) and invoke_body. It never crosses the thunk, a Rust core (a core has always returned before its veneer raises), a JIT or interpreter frame, or a fiber switch. The target is always the innermost protect frame on the raising L's own chain, which is older on the same stack. That also satisfies glibc's fortified __longjmp_chk.\n- The error value is rooted during the jump because zl_pending is a compiled global the collector scans, and nothing allocates during a jump.\n- Nesting: C calls lua_call, which reaches JIT code, which calls C2, and C2 raises. The raise lands in C2's thunk, which returns nil with the error pending. The call returns normally to zlc_call, which returns ZLC_RAISED, and the outer veneer raises into the outer frame.\n- luaL_where(L, 1): lua_getstack and lua_getinfo \"Sl\" answer level 0 as the C function (what \"C\", currentline -1) and level 1 as the Lua caller, from the zl_line (masked by LINE_BITS) and chunk the CFrame captured on entry. Deeper levels come from the debug library when it is on. The \"name\" option answers \"?\" in phase 1 and the call-site name in phase 2.\n- An empty chain (the API called outside any C activation), or lua_error on a State that is not running, calls the lua_atpanic function, prints \"PANIC: unprotected error in call to Lua API (msg)\" and aborts.\n- Pool exhaustion aborts, as elsewhere in zylua; LUA_ERRMEM is never produced.\n\n\nNo Lua-specific collector change. Roots: API stacks and States (reachable from the coroutine record or the main root word), Global (reachable from every State), the registry, C closure upvalues (record cells), and C machine-stack frames (scanned conservatively, including suspended fiber stacks; collect() spills callee-saved registers). Interior pointers keep their block alive, so a string pointer or userdata payload held in a C local keeps its value. The rule for Rust cores: no Rust heap container holds a Lua value across an allocation, because that memory is not scanned. What stays unrooted is what stock Lua also leaves unrooted: C code that stashes value pointers in malloc or static memory, or that calls the API from another thread (the pool's first-foreign-thread rule would turn collection off). Drop analysis: values passed through a function value escape, and a named callee's result is owned only by returns_owned_storage, which an indirect call never meets. So compiled code never releases what C holds; a test pins this. Userdata __gc builds on the finaliser support in w2/L-gc-semantics (7a71d64e, unmerged; gaps in 558e28e): lua_setmetatable marks a userdata whose metatable has __gc, following the reference rule, and finalisers run at program end when the C API is installed. lua_gc maps to the collector and collectgarbage state.\n\n\nlua_yieldk suspends the fiber in place, with the C frames, the protect frame and the thunk kept on the fiber stack. On resume it raises a pending closing marker or error, or computes r (k(L, LUA_YIELD, ctx) when k is given, else the number of resume values) and does zrtl_native_raise(chain, RETURN, r). That discards the C function's frames exactly as 5.4 does. lua_callk and lua_pcallk: when the callee yielded (State.yields moved) and k is set, k runs and returns through a RETURN jump. Without k, the call increments State.nny, and coroutine.yield refuses with \"attempt to yield across a C-call boundary\" when the running record has a State slot whose nny is nonzero. For a program that never loads C, that check is one length test on the record. lua_newthread creates the record with no fiber yet; the first lua_resume binds its body from the bottom of its stack. xmove, status, closethread and resetthread go through the library's coroutine paths.\n\n\n- package.loadlib(path, sym) calls `$LuaC$loadlib(path, sym) -\u003e i64` (0 ok, 1 open, 2 init). The library composes the Lua results from `$LuaC$loaded()` and `$LuaC$error()`: success gives the C function (true for \"*\"), failure gives (nil, message, \"open\" or \"init\"). \"*\" opens with Scope::Global, any other name with Scope::Local. Handles are cached by path in _CLIBS; the first open fixes the scope, as the reference does. \"absent\" is no longer produced.\n- require, after static modules, preload and package.path, and in 5.4's order:\n  - C searcher: zl_searchpath(name, cpath), then `$LuaC$loadfunc(file, name)`.\n  - All-in-one searcher: dotted names only. Search the root before the first dot, then loadfunc(file, fullname). When the function is missing: \"\\n\\tno module 'n' in file 'f'\".\n  - The loader is called with (name, file), and require returns (module, file). Errors are \"error loading module '%s' from file '%s':\\n\\t%s\". Each searcher's \"no file\" lines join the not-found message; attrib checks every path and cpath template appears there.\n- loadfunc: dots become \"_\"; for a name with \"-\", try luaopen_ plus the part before the hyphen (lib2-v2 finds luaopen_lib2), then the part after it.\n- package.cpath: LUA_CPATH_DEFAULT per OS (\"/usr/local/lib/lua/5.4/?.so;/usr/local/lib/lua/5.4/loadall.so;./?.so\" on Unix), overridden by LUA_CPATH_5_4 then LUA_CPATH with \";;\" expanded, and ignored under -E, as package.path is. package.searchers stays the table require checks in phase 1 and becomes four real functions in phase 2.\n- Scope rule (scope.rs): package.loadlib, package.searchers, `package` used as a value, and `require` used as a value set dynamic_code. That gives shared globals and len_meta; unseen_metatables already follows from `package`. An unresolved literal require already shares globals (!all_found in lower.rs). Plain path, cpath, loaded, preload, config and searchpath use changes nothing. As a guard, the program records whether it runs with shared globals, and install refuses (an \"open\" failure naming the reason) if it does not, so a hole in the rule fails loudly.\n\n\nC modules resolve lua_* from the process at dlopen, so zylua exports exactly the generated list.\n- macOS: `_main` plus the names in the existing exported_symbols_list (build.rs emit_link_flags), keeping -dead_strip_dylibs. Modules are built with `-undefined dynamic_lookup` (the LuaRocks convention) and bind in the flat namespace; with only `_main` exported, dlopen fails \"symbol not found in flat namespace\" (scratch-tested).\n- Linux: `-Wl,--dynamic-list=\u003cOUT_DIR\u003e/capi.dynlist` (GNU ld 2.46 on the NUC, and lld), with libzl_capi.a linked `static:+whole-archive`, because --dynamic-list does not pull archive members. Gate: `nm -D zylua` equals the list.\n- Windows (phase 3): /DEF exports from zylua.exe, which produces zylua.lib; the fixtures link against it (lib11 also against lib1.lib). Prebuilt modules import \"lua54.dll\" by name, which an .exe cannot satisfy. Serving them needs a code-free lua54.dll that forwards to zylua.exe. It carries no API code, but it is a DLL, so whether it fits \"not as a dylib\" is the user's call. Without it, modules must be built against zylua.lib.\n- Duplicate plugin symbols: the capi rlib must define no `_zrtl_info` or `_zrtl_symbols` (`nm` gate), and the NUC link is the proof, since macOS links a duplicate clean.\n\n\n- At launch: a few `$LuaC$*` symbols in the existing register_static_plugins batch (still one JIT rebuild). There are no initializers, and exports add no dyld binding work.\n- Steady state: no call path or JIT convention changes. The userdata branches come after the table and string tests. Coroutine records are unchanged (the State slot is appended lazily); coroutine.yield gains one length test in phase 2.\n- The bridge is joined only on the first successful open. That latency is reported separately (6709b37 puts a joined chunk near 1 ms).\n- Gate: release builds, main against branch, interleaved A/B/A/B, at least 300 launches per arm of `zylua run empty.lua` and a hello-world, on the Mac and on the NUC. Report p10, p50 and p90 and peak RSS, plus the export counts (dyld_info -exports, readelf --dyn-syms), and run the Lua benchmark set interleaved for steady state. The medians must differ by less than the A/A spread.\n\n\ntests/conformance.rs builds official/libs before the official category runs, per libs/makefile: `$CC` or `cc`, `-std=gnu99 -O2 -fPIC -shared -I crates/zyntax_lua/capi/vendor`, plus `-undefined dynamic_lookup` on macOS. It produces lib1.so, lib11.so, lib2.so, lib21.so, and lib2-v2.so from lib22.c; libs/.gitignore already ignores them. With no compiler, attrib.lua is reported as skipped with the reason, never counted as passed. attrib.expected is re-pinned from the reference with the libs built: delete attrib.expected, .status and .message, then run with LUA=/opt/homebrew/opt/lua@5.4/bin/lua5.4 (5.4.9; `lua` on PATH is 5.5.1). The reference then reaches OK and exits 0, where today it exits 1. KNOWN_FAILURES replaces `official/attrib.lua 4da50c2` with this issue under the `unix` family until phase 3, so Windows CI reports it as skipped.\n\n\n- sdk/zrtl_native: boundary.c (Unix), Library, export_link_args, and unit tests: a raise through five C frames, a raise after a fiber switch, a nested chain. Plus the compiler convention test on all three tiers. It must build on Windows CI (Library via LoadLibraryExW; the boundary compiles), without the Frame fix.\n- zyntax_lua_capi: vendored headers and lauxlib.c; lapi.c veneers for every export, with stubs raising a clear message; Rust cores for State, Global, stack, index rules, scalars, strings, the thunk, call, pcall, error, registry seeding, light C functions, basic userdata and light userdata, loadlib and loadfunc, and getstack and getinfo \"Sl\" at levels 0 and 1.\n- Implemented lua.h functions, at minimum: the fixtures' closure after macro expansion (lua_gettop, lua_settop, lua_pushvalue, lua_rotate, lua_pushstring, lua_pushfstring, lua_pushvfstring, lua_pushboolean, lua_tointegerx, lua_setglobal, lua_createtable, lua_setfield, lua_pushcclosure, lua_checkstack, lua_version, luaL_checkversion_, luaL_setfuncs, luaL_checkstack). Also absindex, copy, type, typename, the is/to family, rawlen, touserdata, tothread, topointer, the push family, getglobal, gettable, getfield, geti, the raw get and set forms (including p), settable, seti, getmetatable, setmetatable, newuserdatauv, callk and pcallk without continuations, error, rawequal and next. With these, lauxlib's check/opt, error, metatable, udata, ref and setfuncs families work.\n- zyntax_lua: library/capi.rs bridge; the loadlib, C and all-in-one searchers, cpath default and environment in stdlib.rs; USERDATA_KIND and LIGHT_KIND; the lazy State slot; the registry fill; the scope rule and its guard; build.rs exports for macOS and Linux.\n- Harness: the fixture build, the re-pinned attrib, the KNOWN_FAILURES change, and a capi fixture category of small C modules with Lua drivers compared against lua5.4. It covers luaL_error positions, pcall over C, C calling Lua that errors, metamethods in C, upvalues, registry refs, values held only on the API stack and in the registry under ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1, and a C function called from interpreted, Cranelift and LLVM (ZYLUA_LLVM=1) code.\n- Gates: attrib.lua passes on the Mac and the NUC (nucbuild.sh); `nm -D` on the NUC and `dyld_info -exports` on the Mac match the list; `nm` shows no `_zrtl_info` in the capi rlib; the launch and steady-state A/B; clippy -D warnings and fmt clean; Windows CI green with attrib skipped.\n\n\nContinuations and threads (yieldk, callk and pcallk with k, nny, newthread, resume, xmove, status, closethread, isyieldable); toclose and closeslot (luaL_Buffer's box needs them), with tbc slots closed when a frame raises; lua_load from a reader (text only; binary chunks give the reference's message) and lua_dump failing; setwarnf and lua_warning; the debug interface for C (getinfo in full with \"n\" names, getlocal, getupvalue on Lua closures, sethook, upvalueid and upvaluejoin, C frames as what \"C\" and \"=[C]\" in the debug library); userdata __gc on the L-gc-semantics finalisers; the sdk/zrtl TERMINATED string flag; and package.searchers as four real functions that require iterates. Tests: LPeg 1.1 (vendored as a fixture, MIT), with its own test.lua compared against lua5.4; lua-cjson as a second module; every phase-1 gate again, plus GC stress on LPeg.\n\n\n/DEF export and zylua.lib, the Frame-zeroed setjmp, the fixture build against zylua.lib on the Windows CI leg with MSVC- and MinGW-built modules, and the lua54.dll forwarding decision.\n\n\n- A call-convention change that made record code use the Fast convention, or made the LLVM tier call records with fastcc, would break C calls only on Windows x64. The compiler test pins this.\n- Linux: --dynamic-list and whole-archive are unproven here; the NUC gate decides.\n- Refusing yields across lua_call without k matches the reference but adds the check to coroutine.yield; confirm it is unmeasurable in the coroutine benchmarks.\n- A collected coroutine suspended inside C loses its C frames without running them; coroutine.close is the clean path.\n- Building zylua now compiles C through the cc crate, using the C compiler the Rust toolchain already links with. Users running zylua need no compiler.\n- __gc on userdata and finalisers at exit depend on L-gc-semantics landing.","files":null}]}